# Agent Toolbelt routing

Before writing for the user, read
`shared/contracts/references/human-readable-output.md`.
For project documents, also read
`shared/contracts/references/docs-writing.md`.

Use installed agent-toolbelt workflows when the task clearly matches one. Prefer
the matching slash command or skill over re-creating the workflow from memory.
Read the selected skill's `SKILL.md`, then load its references and templates as needed.

Use `/workflow-router` when it is installed and the right workflow is unclear.
Otherwise choose a matching installed skill directly.

Select the lane from the user's request and the target project's requirements.
Existing tickets, feature documents, or available skills alone do not require
a formal lifecycle. Clear bounded changes can use direct implementation and
relevant checks; use phased delivery for coordinated phase/PR work.

Common matches:

- Feature or app work needing brief, flows, acceptance criteria, phased
  implementation, commits, and reviews: `phased-delivery`.
- Project-specific lifecycle, forms, and approvals: follow the target project's workflow owner.
- Code review: `pr-review`.
- Debugging from symptom to fix: `bug-to-fix`.
- Review comment follow-up: `pr-review-reply`.
- Phase gate or merge readiness: `phase-gate`.

General guardrails:

- Respect existing repo instructions and project conventions.
- Keep edits scoped to the user's task.
- Avoid destructive git or filesystem actions unless explicitly requested.
- Run relevant checks when practical; otherwise say what was not run and why.
- Put workflow-specific procedures, rubrics, and checklists in the matching skill
  context instead of applying them to unrelated chats.
